WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.610
If you’re watching this, you probably have
seen at least one video by Business Casual.

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:08.390
The channel is a great example of what’s
possible in terms of business entertainment

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:13.220
content on YouTube and is definitely an influence
on some of my videos, but the channel went

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:17.750
silent about a year ago and I wanted to find
out why, so I dug into it.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:21.190
Even if you’ve never heard of the channel
before, I think you’ll want to stick around

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:26.500
and watch this video, it’s a really interesting
story that highlights a darker side of entrepreneurship

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.320
we don’t often hear about.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.980
Usually, when YouTube channels die off, they
do it slowly.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:36.420
The content gets boring and repetitive and
view numbers start declining, but that’s

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.420
not the case for Business Casual.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:43.579
The last video on the channel got nearly 2
million views and, at 40 minutes long, it

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:45.989
was clearly a ton of work to put together.

00:00:45.989 --> 00:00:50.930
So why would a successful channel like Business
Casual just throw in the towel all of a sudden?

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:55.719
The answer was not what I was expecting at
all and contains a really important lesson

00:00:55.719 --> 00:01:00.140
for entrepreneurs working on any big project,
not just YouTube creators.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:04.670
Long story short, the folks behind Business
Casual are suing YouTube, which is pretty

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:09.439
weird because it seems like YouTube has been
quite good to the channel in general.

00:01:09.439 --> 00:01:14.100
Business Casual has nearly a million subscribers
and none of their content seems to be controversial

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.349
or demonetized.

00:01:15.349 --> 00:01:19.310
But if you scroll through the Business Casual
twitter feed, you will see that whoever is

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:22.930
running the account now is on a crusade against
YouTube.

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:27.859
In fact, the Twitter account has posted basically
the same tweet every day for the last 100

00:01:27.859 --> 00:01:31.140
days straight, so I had to figure out what
was driving this.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:36.060
As a little bit of backstory, Business Casual
was started by this guy, Jordan Kouzmanoff.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.749
He lives in Bulgaria and started the channel
back in 2016 when he was studying business

00:01:40.749 --> 00:01:42.539
at the Rotterdam School of Management.

00:01:42.539 --> 00:01:46.289
I went back through some of his old reddit
posts and he seems like a really genuine guy.

00:01:46.289 --> 00:01:48.849
He’s just super interested in entrepreneurial
stories and explaining business concepts in

00:01:48.849 --> 00:01:49.849
clear and creative ways.

00:01:49.849 --> 00:01:53.100
The channel grew significantly over the past
few years and he was even able to bring on

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:56.030
a narrator and animators to help make the
videos.

00:01:56.030 --> 00:02:00.789
The channel currently has about 80 million
views, which means it easily could have generated

00:02:00.789 --> 00:02:04.649
a million dollars in revenue across YouTube
ads and sponsorship deals.

00:02:04.649 --> 00:02:08.709
But looking at Jordan’s LinkedIn profile,
it looks like he ended his relationship with

00:02:08.709 --> 00:02:11.129
the channel in September of 2019.

00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:13.620
So I started looking into what happened around
that time.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:18.769
I’m not 100% sure, but it looks like he
either sold part or all of the channel to

00:02:18.769 --> 00:02:24.569
a guy named Alex Edson, who runs a company
in Scottsdale, Arizona named eBrands.

00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:28.350
This guy Alex has started a few different
businesses and has been involved with a bunch

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.780
of YouTube channels over the past few years.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:35.019
And it’s Alex’s name on the YouTube lawsuit
that Business Casual is filing.

00:02:35.019 --> 00:02:41.010
So it looks like Alex is really the one driving
everything now and constantly posting to Twitter.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:45.810
Interestingly, the Business Casual twitter
account actually posted the full 243-page

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.519
lawsuit against YouTube, Google, and Alphabet,
and so I read through it to get an idea of

00:02:48.519 --> 00:02:50.189
what’s really going on here.

00:02:50.189 --> 00:02:54.970
Basically, the lawsuit comes down to some
pretty standard copyright infringement claims.

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:59.900
Essentially, it looks like another YouTube
channel took clips from Business Casual videos

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:04.099
and used them in new videos without permission
from the Business Casual team.

00:03:04.099 --> 00:03:07.930
For obvious reasons, I’m not going to show
any of the copyrighted footage, but if you

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:11.340
want to check it out, I’ll link to the clips
in the description below.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:16.299
The actual offending clips are fairly short,
one is just 7 seconds long, and it’s just

00:03:16.299 --> 00:03:19.019
a slow zoom on a public domain image.

00:03:19.019 --> 00:03:23.731
It’s clear that the video was stolen, but
it’s not like they reuploaded a whole video

00:03:23.731 --> 00:03:25.099
or anything like that.

00:03:25.099 --> 00:03:29.510
YouTube actually did take down the videos
that contained the offending clips, but Alex

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:31.749
Edson doesn’t seem to think that’s enough.

00:03:31.749 --> 00:03:33.800
And this is where it gets really interesting.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:38.920
See, the channels that used the copyrighted
footage from Business Casual were affiliated

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.900
with RT, which is a Russian state-controlled
network.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:46.709
RT used to be called Russia Today, and has
been accused of spreading disinformation and

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:48.700
conspiracy theories.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:53.439
In fact, in 2017, RT America was ordered to
register as a foreign agent with the United

00:03:53.439 --> 00:03:55.139
States Department of Justice.

00:03:55.139 --> 00:03:58.810
So when I first saw the tweets from Business
Casual and the lawsuit, I thought that maybe

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:00.400
this was a personal vendetta.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.939
Jordan, the original creator of the channel,
is from Bulgaria after all and Bulgaria’s

00:04:04.939 --> 00:04:08.290
relationship with Russia has been deteriorating
over the past decade.

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:12.959
I assumed that Jordan had taken the copyright
infringement as an opportunity to punish Russia

00:04:12.959 --> 00:04:17.560
and maybe he had some personal family history
with Russia that motivated him to stop making

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:22.310
new videos just to focus on this lawsuit,
but now I think it’s more complicated.

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:26.390
Jordan isn’t listed on any of the lawsuit
filings and it doesn’t look like he’s

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:28.930
affiliated with the channel in a meaningful
way anymore.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:34.490
Alex Edson, on the other hand, is on every
email thread with RT and YouTube representatives

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:37.900
and seems to be the sole driving force behind
the lawsuit.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:42.229
So why does this entrepreneur from Arizona
want to get Russia Today kicked off YouTube

00:04:42.229 --> 00:04:43.229
so badly?

00:04:43.229 --> 00:04:45.500
Well, there are a few factors here.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:47.090
First is the obvious political angle.

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:51.810
Russia has been a major American rival for
the past hundred years and tensions between

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:55.479
Russia and America were particularly high
during the Cold War.

00:04:55.479 --> 00:05:00.710
More recently, Russian interference in the
2016 United States election became an extremely

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:01.770
hot topic.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:06.500
Donald Trump was a deeply unpopular President
throughout his time in office, and many of

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:10.199
his detractors hold Russia accountable for
his election.

00:05:10.199 --> 00:05:12.930
More recently though, the narrative has flipped
a bit.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:17.490
With Trump kicked off of nearly every social
media platform, conservatives have been asking

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:22.300
the question, why is the President of the
United States banned from social media, but

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:23.900
Russia’s state-run account aren’t?

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:28.720
So it’s possible that Alex Edson has a strong
stance here, and wants to get Russia kicked

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:33.100
off of YouTube for political reasons, but
I actually think there’s another layer to

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:34.100
this story…

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:37.789
I’m not a legal expert, but the Business
Casual lawsuit looks weak.

00:05:37.789 --> 00:05:43.629
We’re talking about a few short clips used
for just a few seconds on channels that publish

00:05:43.629 --> 00:05:45.190
dozens of videos per day.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:49.630
YouTube should definitely take them down,
and Business Casual should receive the advertising

00:05:49.630 --> 00:05:53.790
revenue from those videos, since they produced
the original content, but it looks like the

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:58.940
footage was used in the context of a larger
video and the RT channels weren’t directly

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:03.599
reuploading whole videos from Business Casual
in order to steal ad revenue.

00:06:03.599 --> 00:06:08.020
If you consider the actual economic damage
done to the Business Casual channel here,

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:09.100
it looks pretty minimal.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:13.760
The clips were used on Arabic channels, and
Business Casual doesn’t even post content

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:18.020
in Arabic, so there isn’t really a good
argument that people would watch the stolen

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:22.950
videos instead of Business Casual’s original
content, thus depriving them of views and

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:23.950
ad revenue.

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:27.730
But I think Alex is playing a bigger economic
game with this lawsuit.

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:32.419
See, YouTube and really all the big tech companies
are under immense pressure right now from

00:06:32.419 --> 00:06:33.419
the U.S.

00:06:33.419 --> 00:06:34.419
Government.

00:06:34.419 --> 00:06:38.539
As I’ve mentioned in previous videos, both
Elizabeth Warren on the left, and Josh Hawley

00:06:38.539 --> 00:06:41.930
on the right, are calling to break up big
tech companies.

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:46.069
The last thing YouTube needs right now is
a lawsuit gaining steam that makes them look

00:06:46.069 --> 00:06:49.000
like they are helping spread Russian disinformation.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.090
So this case could just get settled out of
court.

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:54.810
Essentially, YouTube might just pay Alex to
go away.

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:57.250
That could be a great outcome for him.

00:06:57.250 --> 00:07:01.030
Even though Business Casual brings in decent
viewer numbers and probably makes thousands

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:05.479
of dollars every month, that content costs
a lot to produce, and getting a big payday

00:07:05.479 --> 00:07:08.810
from YouTube could be significantly more profitable.

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:12.720
Regardless of what happens with the lawsuit,
it looks like Business Casual isn’t working

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:16.639
on any new content at the moment, so if you’re
looking to hear interesting stories about

00:07:16.639 --> 00:07:19.380
successful companies, you should subscribe
to my channel.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:23.980
I just put out a great story about how Discord
grew into a multi-billion dollar company and

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:28.449
next week will be releasing the full story
of Epic Games, who created Fortnite.

00:07:28.449 --> 00:07:33.350
In every video, I try to not only tell the
story of the company in an engaging way, but

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:37.979
also pull out some key takeaways that any
entrepreneur could apply to their own business.

00:07:37.979 --> 00:07:41.410
And there’s a big takeaway here about the
Business Casual story.

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:45.669
Getting copied happens all the time in business,
sometimes it’s a direct copy, and you can

00:07:45.669 --> 00:07:49.560
take legal action, but sometimes it’s just
a competitive business and you can’t do

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:50.650
anything about it.

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:53.439
Regardless, you can’t lose sight of what
you’re building.

00:07:53.439 --> 00:07:58.289
The Business Casual fanbase just wants to
watch engaging documentaries about business,

00:07:58.289 --> 00:08:01.409
and all this focus on suing YouTube doesn’t
help them at all.

00:08:01.409 --> 00:08:05.330
When I started my first company, Soylent,
someone in Russia funnily enough actually

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:09.810
put up a copycat website designed to steal
customer’s credit card information.

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:13.530
People thought they were buying from us, but
they were actually just sending money directly

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:14.569
to these Russian hackers.

00:08:14.569 --> 00:08:19.139
It was annoying, but we didn’t let it distract
us from our mission of making a great product.

00:08:19.139 --> 00:08:23.990
If someone copies you, it might be extremely
frustrating, but you can’t let it consume

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:24.990
you.

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:26.460
You need to stay focused on your business.

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:29.030
There is another option for Business Casual
though.

00:08:29.030 --> 00:08:32.790
One that would let them put pressure on Russian
media while still making great content.

00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:35.290
They could start making videos about this
whole story!

00:08:35.290 --> 00:08:40.080
The most successful channels on YouTube are
personality driven, and I know I would definitely

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:44.280
watch behind the scenes content of Alex Edson’s
fight against Russian media.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:48.120
That would be extremely interesting content
and I think a lot of the Business Casual fanbase

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:49.210
would follow along.

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:53.150
Regardless of what you think about Russia,
or where you stand on copyright infringement,

00:08:53.150 --> 00:08:57.210
I think we can all agree it’s unfortunate
when a channel that produces great content

00:08:57.210 --> 00:08:58.220
just stops.

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:02.300
In the meantime, please consider watching
some of the videos on my channel, the YouTube

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:04.400
algorithm thinks you’ll like this one in
particular.

